    "content": "Ceftriaxone Sodium (Rocephin, Epicephin, Cefaxone, Mesporin, Triaxone) is a Third-Generation Cephalosporin Antibiotic. Indications: Meningitis, severe sepsis, community-acquired pneumonia, hospital-acquired pneumonia, pyelonephritis, gonorrhea, typhoid fever, surgical prophylaxis. Food and Dietary Guidelines: Administered parenterally (IV or IM). Incompatible with calcium-containing intravenous solutions (e.g., Ringer's solution or Hartmann's): forms fatal particulate precipitates in lung and kidney vasculature, particularly in neonates. Contraindications: Severe hypersensitivity to cephalosporins or penicillins (anaphylaxis); hyperbilirubinemic neonates (displaces bilirubin from albumin causing kernicterus); concurrent intravenous calcium in neonates. Severe Drug Interactions: Intravenous calcium (fatal crystalline precipitations in neonates and young infants); Warfarin (may enhance vitamin K antagonism and bleed risk); Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity). Precautions and Administration: No dosage adjustment required in isolated renal or hepatic impairment due to dual excretion routes; reduce dose only if severe combined renal and hepatic failure coexist. Common and Critical Adverse Effects: Diarrhea, injection site pain, biliary sludging / pseudolithiasis (reversible ceftriaxone-calcium salt precipitation), elevated liver enzymes, eosinophilia, thrombocytosis, C. difficile colitis."
