    "precautions": "Narrow therapeutic window. Acute gout dosing: 1.2 mg (or 1 mg) at first sign of flare, followed by 0.6 mg (or 0.5 mg) one hour later (maximum 1.8 mg total in 24 hours). Stop immediately if diarrhea or vomiting occurs.",
    "content": "Colchicine (Goutnil, Colgout, Colcrys, Mitigare) is a Antimitotic Microtubule Assembly Inhibitor. Indications: Treatment of acute gout flares; prophylaxis of gout flares during initiation of urate-lowering therapy; Familial Mediterranean Fever (FMF). Food and Dietary Guidelines: May be taken with or without food. AVOID GRAPEFRUIT AND GRAPEFRUIT JUICE: inhibits CYP3A4, causing dangerous accumulation of colchicine and life-threatening multi-organ failure. Contraindications: Severe renal impairment (CrCl < 30 mL/min) or severe hepatic impairment in patients concurrently receiving strong P-gp or CYP3A4 inhibitors (clarithromycin, ketoconazole, cyclosporine). Severe Drug Interactions: FATAL TOXICITY WARNING: Strong CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein inhibitors (clarithromycin, erythromycin, ketoconazole, itraconazole, cyclosporine) cause fatal colchicine toxicity; Statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin) and fibrates (additive severe myopathy and rhabdomyolysis). Precautions and Administration: Narrow therapeutic window. Acute gout dosing: 1.2 mg (or 1 mg) at first sign of flare, followed by 0.6 mg (or 0.5 mg) one hour later (maximum 1.8 mg total in 24 hours). Stop immediately if diarrhea or vomiting occurs. Common and Critical Adverse Effects: Dose-dependent gastrointestinal toxicity (severe diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps; signals toxicity), bone marrow suppression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia), myopathy and neuropathy."
