    "content": "Iron (Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Polymaltose Complex) (Iberet, Bisleri, Maltofer, Fefol, Fero-Folic, Sangobion) is a Hematinic / Mineral Supplement. Indications: Prevention and treatment of iron-deficiency anemia in pregnancy, chronic blood loss, menorrhagia, malnutrition, and malabsorption. Food and Dietary Guidelines: Ferrous sulfate is best absorbed on an EMPTY STOMACH with water or Vitamin C (orange juice). Taking with food reduces absorption by 40–50% but improves GI tolerance. Iron Polymaltose Complex (IPC) may be taken WITH or immediately after meals without food interference. AVOID TEA, COFFEE, MILK, EGGS, AND CALCIUM SUPPLEMENTS within 2 hours of iron (tannins, phytates, and calcium block absorption). Contraindications: Hemochromatosis, hemosiderosis, hemolytic anemia, thalassemia, active peptic ulcer. Severe Drug Interactions: Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) and Tetracyclines (doxycycline): iron binds antibiotics forming unabsorbable chelates (SEPARATE BY AT LEAST 2 TO 4 HOURS); Levothyroxine (iron decreases thyroid hormone absorption; separate by 4 hours); Antacids and PPIs (reduce iron absorption). Precautions and Administration: Warn patients that black stools are expected and harmless, but ensure it is not confused with melena. Keep out of reach of children (acute iron overdose in toddlers is fatal). Common and Critical Adverse Effects: Constipation, dark or black-colored stools (harmless oxidation of unabsorbed iron), nausea, epigastric distress, metallic taste, vomiting."
