    "content": "Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) (Indrop-D, Sunny-D, Calci-D, D-3, D-Cure, Vigantol) is a Fat-Soluble Secosteroid Vitamin. Indications: Treatment and prevention of Vitamin D deficiency, rickets, osteomalacia, osteoporosis adjunctive therapy, hypoparathyroidism. Food and Dietary Guidelines: TAKE WITH A MEAL CONTAINING DIETARY FAT: As a fat-soluble vitamin, oral bioavailability increases by up to 30–50% when taken with lipid-containing foods (milk, yogurt, eggs, olive oil). Contraindications: Hypercalcemia; hypervitaminosis D; calcium renal lithiasis; severe renal osteodystrophy with hyperphosphatemia. Severe Drug Interactions: Thiazide diuretics (concurrent use increases risk of hypercalcemia); Digoxin (hypercalcemia potentiates digitalis cardiotoxicity); Mineral oil, orlistat, and cholestyramine (impair fat-soluble vitamin absorption; separate by at least 2 hours); Phenytoin and carbamazepine (induce CYP enzymes, accelerating Vitamin D degradation). Precautions and Administration: In South Asia, high-dose ampoules (e.g. 200,000 IU or 600,000 IU oral) are commonly prescribed monthly or weekly: verify that high doses are taken on schedule and not taken daily to avoid catastrophic hypercalcemic crisis. Common and Critical Adverse Effects: None at nutritional doses. Vitamin D toxicity (hypervitaminosis D) causes hypercalcemia: nausea, vomiting, polyuria, polydipsia, weakness, constipation, nephrocalcinosis, cardiac arrhythmias."
